### 2. Architecture of the New `src/paths.rs` Module

#### 2.1 Struct Design: `BivvyDirs`

A struct that holds all resolved base directories. This enables testability by allowing construction with injected paths rather than relying on global `dirs::*` calls.

```rust
// src/paths.rs

use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};

/// Resolved base directories for all Bivvy user-global data.
///
/// Centralizes path resolution so every module uses the same source of truth.
/// Supports XDG base directories with fallback to legacy `~/.bivvy` paths
/// for backward compatibility.
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct BivvyDirs {
    /// XDG_CONFIG_HOME/bivvy (config files)
    config: PathBuf,
    /// XDG_DATA_HOME/bivvy (state, projects, templates, reports)
    data: PathBuf,
    /// XDG_CACHE_HOME/bivvy (template cache, update checks)
    cache: PathBuf,
}
```

#### 2.2 Constructor Logic

```rust
impl BivvyDirs {
    /// Resolve directories using standard XDG locations.
    ///
    /// This is the primary constructor for production use.
    pub fn resolve() -> Option<Self> { ... }

    /// Create with explicit base directories (for testing).
    pub fn with_roots(config: PathBuf, data: PathBuf, cache: PathBuf) -> Self { ... }
}
```

The `resolve()` constructor:
1. Check for `BIVVY_HOME` env var. If set, use it as a single root for all categories (config, data, cache all under `$BIVVY_HOME/`). This provides an escape hatch for testing, CI, and advanced users.
2. Otherwise, use `dirs::config_dir()`, `dirs::data_dir()`, `dirs::cache_dir()`, each with `/bivvy` appended.
3. Return `None` only if the underlying `dirs` calls all fail (extremely rare).

#### 2.3 Path Accessor Methods with Legacy Fallback

Each accessor method implements the same fallback pattern:

```rust
